// Generated by moonbit-community/embed from ./internal/docs/mdfiles/quickstart.md.

///|
let _embed_quickstart_md : String =
  #|# Quickstart: your first CLI test
  #|
  #|Mooncram tests MoonBit command-line programs using commands and expected output
  #|written in Markdown. It supports these targets:
  #|
  #|| Target | Command inside a `mooncram` block | Prerequisites | Backend |
  #|| --- | --- | --- | --- |
  #|| Standalone script | `moonx ./hello.mbtx` | An existing `.mbtx` file, relative to the block's working directory or absolute. No module declaration is needed. | Always Wasm. |
  #|| Local executable package | `moonx user/acme/cmd/tool` | A `mooncram user/acme` fence; launch from the directory whose `moon.mod` declares that exact name. The package's `moon.pkg` must declare `pkgtype(kind: "executable")` or `options("is-main": true)`. | Wasm by default; `--target native` selects native. |
  #|| Remote executable package | `moonx user/tool[/package]@1.2.3` | A published executable with an exact SemVer version; `moonx` fetches and caches it. No module declaration is needed. | Always Wasm. |
  #|
  #|The package names above illustrate the required form. Arbitrary binaries,
  #|ordinary shell commands such as `echo` or `cat`, and `.mbt` files are not
  #|supported targets. Every pipeline segment must start with `moonx` after any
  #|temporary environment assignments. Commands do not run through a shell:
  #|variables, wildcards and `~` are not expanded, and redirection is unsupported.
  #|
  #|## Before executing cases
  #|
  #|| Decision | Rule |
  #|| --- | --- |
  #|| Assertions | By default, assert stdout and program exit code `0`. Stderr needs explicit paired `@STDOUT` and `@STDERR` markers. |
  #|| Paths | Local modules use `moon.mod` directly in the startup directory, without searching parents. Scripts and relative file arguments use the block's working directory: the canonical document's parent by default, or its `#cwd` directory. `#cwd` does not change module lookup. |
  #|| Execution | `test`, `update`, and `update --dry-run` execute code with your permissions, without a sandbox or temporary workspace isolation. Dry-run leaves Markdown unchanged, but programs can still modify files. Use `check` to validate without execution. |
  #|| Timeout | The default 60000 ms deadline is shared by each case's builds, remote fetching, execution, and output capture. |
  #|
  #|## Create two files
  #|
  #|Have `mooncram` installed, with `moon`, `moonx` and `moonrun` on `PATH`.
  #|This example needs no repository checkout, module configuration or external
  #|dependencies. Create the following files in any working directory:
  #|
  #|```text
  #|demo/
  #|└── tests/
  #|    ├── hello.mbtx
  #|    └── hello.md
  #|```
  #|
  #|Complete contents of `tests/hello.mbtx`:
  #|
  #|```mbt
  #|fn main {
  #|  println("Hello, MoonBit!")
  #|}
  #|```
  #|
  #|Complete contents of `tests/hello.md`:
  #|
  #|````markdown
  #|# Greeting
  #|
  #|The script greets the user.
  #|
  #|```mooncram
  #|$ moonx ./hello.mbtx
  #|Hello, MoonBit!
  #|```
  #|````
  #|
  #|The `$ ` line starts a case. Following lines assert stdout; the default expected
  #|program exit code is `0`. Stderr is not asserted unless you explicitly use both
  #|`@STDOUT` and `@STDERR` markers. Only backtick fences labeled `mooncram` are
  #|executed; when creating `hello.md`, omit the outer four-backtick `markdown`
  #|wrapper shown here.
  #|
  #|## Check and run
  #|
  #|From `demo`, run these commands in your terminal:
  #|
  #|```sh
  #|mooncram check tests/hello.md
  #|mooncram test tests/hello.md
  #|```
  #|
  #|`check` prints the following summary and exits with `0`:
  #|
  #|```text
  #|1 cases, 0 errors
  #|```
  #|
  #|`test` builds and executes the script, prints the following summary and exits
  #|with `0`:
  #|
  #|```text
  #|1 cases, 0 failed, 0 errors
  #|```
  #|
  #|There is no individual report for a passing case. The input `tests/hello.md`
  #|is relative to the directory where you launch mooncram. The script
  #|`./hello.mbtx` is relative to the document directory, `demo/tests`.
  #|The startup directory need not contain `moon.mod` for this script-only test.
  #|
  #|`check` validates structure and current targets, but does not compile or test
  #|behavior. A script generated by an earlier case can be reported missing by
  #|`check`; `test` resolves targets as cases run.
  #|
  #|Mooncram's own exit code is independent of the expected program exit code.
  #|A program returning `2` can pass a case ending in `[2]`. `test` returns `0`
  #|when all cases pass, `1` for mismatches without errors, and `2` for errors or
  #|no cases.
  #|
  #|## Handle a changed result
  #|
  #|If the greeting changes unexpectedly, fix the program and rerun `test`.
  #|For an intentional change, keep these update rules in mind:
  #|
  #|- Updates also process passing cases and may normalize expectations and fences.
  #|  Review the full proposed diff.
  #|- An execution error anywhere in a document prevents all its expectation
  #|  updates and dry-run document diffs. Other documents can still update.
  #|- `update --dry-run` can print `FAIL` and return `0`. This means the update
  #|  preview completed without errors; use `test` for the CI pass/fail decision.
  #|
  #|Preview the new expectations, review the diff, apply it, and verify again:
  #|
  #|```sh
  #|mooncram update tests/hello.md --dry-run
  #|mooncram update tests/hello.md
  #|mooncram test tests/hello.md
  #|```
  #|
  #|Keep any nondefault `--target` and `--timeout-ms` options the same for `test`,
  #|dry-run and `update`. The final `test` must pass to confirm the updated examples.
  #|

///|
pub let quickstart_md : String = _embed_quickstart_md
